Create an example demonstrating the automatic generation of a table of contents in a LaTeX document, ensuring to include all required commands within your example. 

The corresponding Golden Response would be:
To generate an automatic table of contents in a LaTeX document, you can use the `\tableofcontents` command. Here is an example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
This is the introduction section.

\section{Methodology}
This section describes the methodology.

\section{Results}
This section presents the results.

\section{Conclusion}
This is the conclusion section.

\end{document}
```

In this example, the `\tableofcontents` command is used to automatically generate the table of contents, and each section in the document is properly labeled with a section command (`\section{}`).

